# (2) Dickson, Deep-Sea Res. 37 (1990) 755-766, Table 3, p. 762: ln(K*/m0) = -19.7556 for the dissociation of
#     boric acid in synthetic seawater of S = 35 (composition of Table 1, p. 756: the totals of this operation) at
#     298.15 K, on the TOTAL hydrogen ion scale, molality (mol per kg of water); stated uncertainty +-0.004 in ln K*
#     (p. 764).  The stoichiometric constant lumps every orthoborate form, so the engine's B(OH)4- plus the
#     CaB(OH)4+ and MgB(OH)4+ pairs are the numerator.  CONVERSION to what the engine computes: the engine's pH
#     is the FREE hydrogen ion; the paper's scale counts the proton bound to sulfate, m*(H) = m(H) + m(HSO4)
#     (the paper's eq. 10 with the bisulfate constant of the medium).  Bisulfate is therefore in this operation's
#     network and the engine's own m(HSO4) is used: no sulfate constant from outside the engine, and the paper
#     says that constant is hard to know accurately in seawater, so the conversion carries its own
#     uncertainty (see below).  Arithmetic, from the run:
#         m*(H) = 8.5661e-9 + 2.6142e-9 = 1.1180e-8 mol/kg ;  K* = exp(-19.7556) = 2.6318e-9
#         R = K*/m*(H) = 0.23539 ;  m_H3BO3 = B_T/(1+R) = 3.4901e-4 mol/kg, B_T = 4.3116e-4.
#     The model gives ln K* = -19.7115 (0.044 above the paper), i.e. m_H3BO3 0.85 % below the value the paper's
#     constant implies.  TOLERANCE 1.2e-2 on m_H3BO3 (m_H3BO3 moves by 0.2 x the change in ln K*, so it is +-0.06 in
#     ln K*): the root-sum-square of the paper's +-0.004 (p. 764), the fit residual 0.0042 (p. 763), the engine's
#     bisulfate against a different constant (0.018 in ln K*, the size of the difference between the engine's
#     m(HSO4)/m(H) = 0.305 and the 0.2815 that Dickson's constant gives) and the borate paper's own emf scatter
#     for the Ca and Mg terms (0.013-0.025 in ln gamma, its p. 2773) is about 0.03; the tolerance is twice that.
#     The model sits outside the paper's stated uncertainty (0.044 against 0.004) and inside the tolerance.
#     INDEPENDENCE: PARTLY.  Dickson's seawater measurements are not among the data the borate paper says it
#     fitted (its p. 2773: Platford's isopiestic data and Hershey et al. 1985 emf data in NaCl, CaCl2 and MgCl2
#     for the Na, Ca and Mg terms; Dickson's UNPUBLISHED emf data in KCl, same laboratory and cell type, for the
#     K terms), but the Na, Ca and Mg borate terms rest on data of the same family, and the major-ion terms of
#     the medium come from Harvie, Moller & Weare 1984.
